    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, so descriptive context is better than just a blank field. But sometimes the source is totally mysterious, like a map found in a random collection with no provenance at all. In those cases where the source feels almost intentionally obscured or just completely inexplicable, is there any academic way to categorize that \"unidentifiable\" nature? Like, how does an archivist label something that seems to have appeared out of nowhere without making it sound like a mystery novel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For truly inexplicable items lacking any trace of origin, the best practice is to use precise archival terminology within the `dc:coverage` or `dc:description` facets. Labeling it as \"Object of Unverified Provenance\" or \"Source Undetermined\" clearly communicates the archival uncertainty to the researcher. This terminology frames the lack of data as a factual condition of the artifact, rather than an editorial guess about its backstory. This maintains scholarly rigor by documenting the limits of current knowledge.",
      "source": "target",
